#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172722} :activity/id #uuid "5ba01719-f8b9-5679-8758-86e56046fcdf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050936 :topic/id #uuid "72cbcd7e-0332-5490-aae5-4381fdc60e68" :topic/math-academy-id 3300} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173212 :tutorial/content "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election. \n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3.\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p.$ \n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p,$ the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A.$ We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b64af41f-7266-5dc3-9360-aa6c591384e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6254 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89f797af-a45c-58a5-8bec-5490a8e51df2" :step/math-academy-id 176376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173223 :tutorial/content "We have ![](images/4a/4a616e999005a19046f642d63f02ed9d7054edec758f6a03edb7eb90c29e8713.svg) and we wish to construct a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of ![](images/be/be3d0efacea6b66b85bae982c4d923f2473fe722d5f30c4b9a940ac67773cff2.svg)\n\nSince the United States has around ![](images/59/592c5e42c05234443b91e6d53afea4f21fb7e5dfb0ab1e645fce7ffe881bfc9c.svg) million registered voters, the sample size ![](images/35/3597ee702449dc99d1b986728db87df4b1e6ee3f2ff01bf7db08ad6c48a9bd42.svg) is clearly less than ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n![](images/24/24bc598b02901990c48811d40a22934c7f4d59be5e57bcb7f8ac6ca592d1abcb.svg)\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) as an estimate for ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg)):\n\n![](images/bf/bfd75db155b00910cb00aa4c37df692c254df765ec94ba265af589f72868071d.svg)\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable. \n\nBy transforming ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n![](images/c4/c461c3f9d172553d6893ddd8bb9f5f9490ba9b3135de8093a367d5286e306344.svg)\n\nSince the true population proportion ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is unknown, we substitute in this formula the sample proportion ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) for ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/e7/e7867d67e36fcd33810b482ab26bdfe4be2da4549463e8f1602c65e5a3ae78a0.svg)\n\nWe wish to find a ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-interval that we're ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confident that the random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter ![](images/b2/b2382453bd24fb0facae9b8be51cbdb4f3606603ab108670fde4f7815fb5d7a4.svg) Since we're computing a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval, we have \n\n![](images/7e/7e6fa76dc615bf00f8cbdf957c653ba7b6217fda2dab0937b1e0cc0741cb7b61.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/28/28f62d4b860fec1ef5c6a7016529d78c308813779a7f12147e508bce162315a7.svg) is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as ![](images/42/420634552d23ada1156e47b7a39985de21735a4d652674e15a481ef25cbf1c37.svg)\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n![](images/48/4875e8608b48eb4fd92e20e26114baf19acb4257cc077d08581483181274d3cc.svg)  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that ![](images/74/7437fff22f0f020a39b79d5cb1470b545ba00b61f271a4ac9ee2778c7a549fe2.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/b9/b9ce27b3145e86201f04307c54d83681d016c11a60bd04a8159ca81b202c2bd1.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n![](images/c9/c9a4066e2b0ed64d8e64b524342b2c875a7bdc62f2eede2c4fb4a96354498ee4.svg)  \n\nTherefore, there is a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) probability that our random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) will lie in the interval ![](images/b6/b62822fdda83ec83c86e736d28f8977e169d277b0a845bef6422a8feed2500d7.svg)\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population proportion ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/06/06861abae3aa843df157b835d2505f1e11b1b72108e482a492b98374a395cf14.svg)   \n\nTherefore, a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/7e/7ea325842297236a6ed3c3704eb8fa3b6d3c4214d14fc6548c83a63e9d3e2f50.svg)\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n![](images/50/5044d5c3f2e8014d52f939897815813472d6010fe5b9f0903ca9e1fa2692420a.svg)\n\nwe obtain that our ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is \n\n![](images/95/95d52028e8b20a7ad509479ea958f31b698f3b74b8c24088ed68dd88bd3d0c90.svg) \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n![](images/03/03e447d7a11205c8684e92a64b58d7db4e9f8de019c6f99af395211b46e42fdf.svg)\n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "462ecbef-51e0-5a01-808e-cd71bd17cbc2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6462 :tutorial/title "Constructing Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "413a1302-d708-5a68-8969-eee6f8bf3793" :step/math-academy-id 176377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173213 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/d0/d07c13f30dc4a0960b70f2705bb615f79c0c7ef3a6ed5d7a8eef2401db02d6c2.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe719d03-f27c-50c1-8c5d-9bf9e9326e10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6255 :tutorial/title "Confidence Intervals for Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "d86369dd-0073-526c-baf8-4f7a189e8246" :step/math-academy-id 176378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171681 :question/id #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" :question/math-academy-id "e-10051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524034}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "2700b310-2c62-5ffd-ba65-f8b258d201e3" :step/math-academy-id 176379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171682 :question/id #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" :question/math-academy-id "e-10054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523949}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"} :step/id #uuid "822975a7-7bca-5cc6-9812-46e5051e550a" :step/math-academy-id 176382}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172149}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524060} :activity/id #uuid "23e61e75-beb0-5233-84f0-0d07934eb397" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050936 :topic/id #uuid "72cbcd7e-0332-5490-aae5-4381fdc60e68" :topic/math-academy-id 3300} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523359} {:answer/id #uuid "49b3faa2-261e-5b23-a9d3-453a88a3fc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523405} {:answer/id #uuid "4d90ca59-9e8e-5ca6-9c41-334319a81f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523413} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d1902-3b9f-5884-a0ff-1cb6fa586fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523732} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9b0397-2df1-5c1b-bc5c-68b1434b1f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523359} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03129ca-31a0-5c5b-be36-c6ef3c14bdc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4c85404-18e8-5990-bb12-ba8d0d33ef92" :question/math-academy-id "q-142713" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.7,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.75,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=80\\cdot 0.7=56>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=80\\cdot (1-0.7)=24>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.084\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "545b80a9-b6e1-5d1f-869a-37ad814aa7f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245260ce-f9eb-5103-abac-929724075c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523326} {:answer/id #uuid "341d3433-7189-5f66-87a1-9332a008905d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523363} {:answer/id #uuid "51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523418} {:answer/id #uuid "b891f11a-a92a-540d-8453-b59eaffc3349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523640} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5679a7-92b1-5e2b-b3f0-4824eb166850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523418} :answer-field/id #uuid "acae107c-a0bc-5d9a-b751-b05730ad5a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" :question/math-academy-id "q-143381" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=160$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=5%=0.05,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=160\\cdot 0.05=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=160\\cdot (1-0.05)=152>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.05\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{160}} \\\\  & 0.05\\pm 0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55ab25b8-178a-5f6f-9fb3-b2f790774916"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd6bc15-cdc3-53a9-b331-5c5176f9dd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523414} {:answer/id #uuid "b094ffa0-7616-5773-817f-21c3ca2b8253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523625} {:answer/id #uuid "ded03ed4-9849-5189-9368-6cbd92efb18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523722} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523752} {:answer/id #uuid "f880901b-d44c-5e44-aab2-24fe5ad46d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523752} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b3069-b6b7-5440-a62a-32c4be352c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-143320" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=75$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "683d3fd0-7aa6-5a47-b0f5-2215480efbbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0059189e-a1d5-5009-984c-ab56aaf9c32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523250} {:answer/id #uuid "06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523269} {:answer/id #uuid "91e6c7ed-6456-52e2-b6b3-ca05ed0e1fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523561} {:answer/id #uuid "cff2a8a8-d184-58b6-9e13-e0ce109be435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523692} {:answer/id #uuid "fbec8e4e-7d76-5bea-a85e-7474bdb2c704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523269} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ab0739-3e4c-54dd-b8d9-d593dca45aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" :question/math-academy-id "q-143308" :question/problem "A poll of $800$ randomly selected U.S. Internet subscribers reveals that only $560$ of those interviewed are satisfied with their current internet provider. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. internet users that are satisfied with their current internet providers.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "76cd70fa-6d63-54f7-87b7-85e928a759ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa3363d-ab33-5aa9-af84-d3b6a64ef0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.40\\pm 0.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523306} {:answer/id #uuid "25aeefc7-0d24-5ed3-a18a-7d64cdb2b831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523328} {:answer/id #uuid "4c12b846-20ca-5213-95bb-ffa4ca0fbb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.64\\pm 0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523409} {:answer/id #uuid "e296f059-0c25-50c1-9765-f7943410911b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523729} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523765} :answer-field/id #uuid "537466c1-89bf-5f49-8a56-e0b12cdda1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4a59a953-fd66-5704-998f-ee025eba0b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143305" :question/problem "A survey conducted by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all college students in the US,\n\n- some of the students surveyed love soccer, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who love soccer can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{40}{125}=0.32\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.32=40>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.32)=85>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.32\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.32\\pm 0.107\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f5a2e185-d130-54a1-8f2f-7d0113c88c8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524071}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523238}]}
